@*
    The other half of a file pane: the file itself, in the one scroll region the
    reader can see.

    Four things can be in it and only ever one at a time — the file rendered as
    markdown, the file shown as code, two versions of it aligned by heading, or a
    body the host brought itself. Which one is the pane's decision, arriving here
    already made: ShowsComparison, SuppliedBody and RendersMarkdown are answers and
    not questions. The header's controls and this region have to agree about which
    mode the pane is in, and the way to guarantee that is for neither of them to
    decide it.

    Nothing here reads the file either. The blocks arrive parsed, the frontmatter
    arrives read, the comparison arrives aligned — all three are cached against the
    text they came from, and a file open in an editor re-renders on every
    keystroke. Parsing in a child would throw that caching away and do the whole
    file's worth of work per character.

    What is genuinely this component's is the region itself: its class, its tab
    stop, its accessible name and how tall it may grow before it scrolls. Those
    four are one decision made four ways — see the comment on the element — and
    they belong wherever the element does.

    The frontmatter strip is here rather than in the header for the reason recorded
    against it: it is the content saying what it is for, not the line that
    identifies the file. It sits above the scroll region and outside it, so it does
    not scroll away either.

    ---

    The markup below is indented as though it were still inside FileView's
    `<article>`, and it has to stay that way — see FileHeader for the argument.
    Indentation between two sibling elements is a text node in the rendered
    output, so re-indenting this file would change the bytes every host ships.
*@

    @if (!Frontmatter.IsEmpty)
    {
        @* The file's own frontmatter, shown as the facts it is.

           Its own strip under the header rather than another row inside it. The
           header is two columns that already crowd each other when the pane is
           narrow, and a sentence describing the whole file is not part of the
           line that identifies it — the name and the path are what a reader
           checks before trusting the content, and this is the content telling
           them what it is for.

           Only when asked. A file whose first lines nobody has offered to read
           renders exactly as it did before this existed, block for block. *@
        <div class="file-view__frontmatter" data-testid="@Suffix("frontmatter")">
            @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Frontmatter.Description))
            {
                <p class="file-view__frontmatter-description" data-testid="@Suffix("description")">@Frontmatter.Description</p>
            }

            @if (Frontmatter.ApplyTo.Count > 0)
            {
                @* One badge per glob, and a short label in front of them: a row of
                   paths with nothing naming it reads as a breadcrumb. *@
                <div class="file-view__frontmatter-row" data-testid="@Suffix("applies-to")">
                    <span class="file-view__frontmatter-label">Applies to</span>
                    @foreach (var glob in Frontmatter.ApplyTo)
                    {
                        <Badge Kind="glob">@glob</Badge>
                    }
                </div>
            }

            @if (Frontmatter.Tools.Count > 0)
            {
                <div class="file-view__frontmatter-row" data-testid="@Suffix("tools")">
                    <span class="file-view__frontmatter-label">Tools</span>
                    @foreach (var tool in Frontmatter.Tools)
                    {
                        <Badge Kind="tool">@tool</Badge>
                    }
                </div>
            }

            @* Everything else the block states, as its own key and its own words,
               in the order the file wrote them.

               The alternative is what this used to do: draw the three keys it
               recognises and take the rest out of the body along with them. A
               `name` on a skill file and a `mode` on a prompt then left the file
               without being shown anywhere, and the author of that line had no way
               to tell whether the file or the viewer had lost it. So the rule is
               the strip never hides what it does not show — which makes the
               unrecognised key a row rather than a reason to keep the noise.

               No badge and no second visual language for them: the label is the
               key as the file spells it and the value is text, in the same row the
               two lists above use. A badge says "one of a set", and a key nothing
               here recognises is not known to be one of anything. *@
            @foreach (var field in Frontmatter.Other)
            {
                <div class="file-view__frontmatter-row" data-testid="@Suffix($"field-{field.Key.ToLowerInvariant()}")">
                    <span class="file-view__frontmatter-label">@field.Label</span>
                    <span class="file-view__frontmatter-value">@field.Text</span>
                </div>
            }
        </div>
    }

    @* A scroll container is an interactive element even though it is a div:
       without tabindex a keyboard user cannot reach the overflow in Chromium at
       all (.design/accessibility.md, "Non-native controls"). role + name keep it
       from being an unlabelled tab stop.

       A supplied body drops that tabindex, the way FolderView's body already
       does and for the same reason. The rule is about elements that become the
       only way to reach something: the built-in shapes render no controls at
       all, so the region is how a keyboard gets at the overflow, but a host
       supplies a body because it has controls to put in it and those are tab
       stops already. Leaving the stop in would put it in front of the editor,
       where it announces the pane and then does nothing on the way to typing. A
       host that supplies a body with nothing focusable in it and lets it
       overflow owns making that reachable — the same bargain FolderView takes.
       The role and the name are not conditional either way.

       The class is computed for a second reason, and only while the host's
       editor is what is on screen: a rendered file is as tall as the file, and a
       textarea is as tall as its row count. Reading, the body's own content
       fills the pane; editing, it stops at row fourteen and leaves the rest of
       the pane empty underneath. So the body says it is holding an editor —
       see ShowsEditBody — and the stylesheet lets whatever is inside it stretch
       to the room the pane gave, and gives up the padding and the scrollbar
       gutter it holds prose off its edges with. An editor pads its own text and
       scrolls inside its own box; the same inset around it reads as a control
       floating in the pane rather than the pane being the control. *@
    <div class="@BodyClass"
         role="region"
         tabindex="@BodyTabIndex"
         aria-label="@BodyLabel"
         style="@BodyStyle"
         data-testid="@BodyTestId">
        @if (ShowsComparison)
        {
            @if (SelectedBaseline?.Unavailable is { } unavailable)
            {
                <EmptyState Title="Nothing to compare against"
                            Description="@unavailable"
                            ActionCssClass="@null"
                            CssClass="file-view__empty"
                            TestId="@Suffix("compare-unavailable")" />
            }
            else
            {
                @* Bare: this pane's header already says which file it is and this
                   body already scrolls. See CodeView below — same reason, same
                   bargain. *@
                <MarkdownCompareView Section="@Comparison"
                                     Bare="true"
                                     FileName="@Name"
                                     BeforeLabel="@(SelectedBaseline?.Label ?? "Before")"
                                     AfterLabel="@AfterLabel"
                                     TestId="@Suffix("compare-view")" />
            }
        }
        else if (SuppliedBody is { } supplied)
        {
            @supplied
        }
        else if (RendersMarkdown)
        {
            @* Everything the file says about itself, drawn from the file: a
               chapter's status where its heading is, a diagram where its fence
               is, and a copy button for each chapter beside the heading that
               names it. A host that wants any of those asks for them here rather
               than parsing the same text a second time to draw them around the
               outside — which is what the knowledge panes were doing, and why a
               chapter's diagrams used to appear below the file that contained
               them. *@
            <MarkdownView Blocks="@Blocks"
                          Source="@MarkdownSource"
                          AllowChapterCopy="@AllowChapterCopy"
                          RenderKnowledgeMetadata="@RenderKnowledgeMetadata"
                          RenderFileKnowledgeMetadata="false"
                          RenderKnowledgeMetadataFields="@RenderKnowledgeMetadataFields"
                          KnowledgeFolder="@KnowledgeFolder"
                          KnowledgeDocumentPath="@KnowledgeDocumentPath"
                          OnKnowledgeStatusChanged="@OnKnowledgeStatusChanged"
                          OnKnowledgeNavigate="@OnKnowledgeNavigate"
                          KnowledgeHrefFor="@KnowledgeHrefFor"
                          Comments="@Comments"
                          Layout="@BodyCommentLayout"
                          OnAddComment="@OnAddComment"
                          OnResolveComment="@OnResolveComment"
                          OnEditComment="@OnEditComment" />
        }
        else
        {
            @* Numbered because this is a file and not a snippet: the reason to
               look at source in a pane like this is usually to say where in it
               something is. *@
            <CodeView Source="@Body"
                      Language="@Language"
                      Bare="true"
                      ShowLineNumbers="true"
                      CssClass="file-view__code" />
        }
    </div>
@code {
    /// <summary>The file's name — the name of the scroll region, and half of what
    /// it is called while two versions are in it.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public string Name { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>The pane's test id. The region takes <c>-body</c> and everything
    /// inside derives its own; null leaves them all off.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// The file's leading YAML frontmatter, already read, drawn as a strip above
    /// the scroll region: what the file says it is about, the globs and tools it
    /// names, and every other key it states as a plain row.
    /// <para>
    /// <see cref="MarkdownFrontmatter.None"/> — the default — draws nothing, and
    /// that is also what a host that never asked for the strip hands in. One value
    /// rather than a value and a flag: "read nothing" and "read a block that
    /// states nothing" are the same answer here, and the pane has already made
    /// them the same answer upstream.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Every key is drawn, which is what makes it safe for the pane to hand
    /// <see cref="MarkdownSource"/> in without those lines: nothing leaves the body
    /// that the strip does not show.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public MarkdownFrontmatter Frontmatter { get; set; } = MarkdownFrontmatter.None;

    /// <summary>Whether the comparison is what the region shows: asked for, and
    /// something to answer with. The pane's answer — it takes precedence over both
    /// of the other two, and only the pane can see all three.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool ShowsComparison { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The version being compared against — its label names the left-hand
    /// side, and its reason, when it has one, is drawn in place of the comparison.
    /// Already resolved out of the pane's list.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public FileCompareBaseline? SelectedBaseline { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The two versions, already aligned by heading. Held and handed down
    /// rather than derived here: aligning two documents walks both of them, and a
    /// pane re-renders every time a fold in it opens.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public ComparedSection? Comparison { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What the version on screen is called in the comparison. The file
    /// as it is now, which is the reader's own copy of it.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string AfterLabel { get; set; } = "Now";

    /// <summary>
    /// The body the host is drawing, if it is the host's turn.
    /// <para>
    /// One fragment and not two, because which of the host's two bodies won —
    /// its editor while editing, or the body it supplies for every mode — is a
    /// precedence the pane already resolved. Null means it is not the host's turn
    /// at all, and this renders the file itself.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? SuppliedBody { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether what is on screen is specifically the host's <em>editing</em> body —
    /// the one a reader got to by pressing Edit.
    /// <para>
    /// It changes the region's class and nothing else. A rendered file is as tall
    /// as the file and a textarea is as tall as its row count, so the editing
    /// modifier is what lets the stylesheet stretch whatever is inside to the room
    /// the pane gave, and give up the padding and scrollbar gutter it holds prose
    /// off its edges with.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool ShowsEditBody { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether the file is read as prose or as code. The pane's decision,
    /// made from the file's own name — see <c>FileView.RendersMarkdown</c>, which
    /// records why markdown is the fallback rather than an entry on a
    /// list.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool RendersMarkdown { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The file's blocks, already parsed. Empty whenever the pane is not
    /// rendering markdown, which is also when nothing here reads them.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public IReadOnlyList<MdBlock> Blocks { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>The text <see cref="Blocks"/> were parsed from, handed over beside
    /// them because it is what a chapter copy slices: the copies address this
    /// string by the offsets those blocks carry, so the other text would copy the
    /// wrong lines. It is the file without the lines the frontmatter strip is
    /// showing, when it is showing any.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? MarkdownSource { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The file's contents, verbatim — what a code file is shown as. Not
    /// read when the body is markdown or the host's.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Body { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What <see cref="Body"/> is written in, already resolved from the
    /// caller's answer or the file's name. A language the highlighter has no
    /// grammar for still renders as code, in one colour.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Language { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether a chapter's heading offers to copy that chapter. Passed
    /// straight to the read view, which explains what it costs to get
    /// wrong.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool AllowChapterCopy { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether a fenced <c>meta</c> block is read as the knowledge record
    /// it is, putting each chapter's status beside its heading. Passed to the read
    /// view. The file's own block is not among them: the pane has taken it into
    /// the header and tells the read view to leave it out.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool RenderKnowledgeMetadata { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether a chapter's record draws the fields under the status too.
    /// Passed straight to the read view.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool RenderKnowledgeMetadataFields { get; set; } = true;

    /// <summary>Which knowledge folder the file was read from, so a chapter's
    /// status can be checked against that folder's vocabulary.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public KnowledgeFolder KnowledgeFolder { get; set; } = KnowledgeFolder.Unknown;

    /// <summary>The repository-relative path of the knowledge document being
    /// shown, which is what a relative link in the body resolves against.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? KnowledgeDocumentPath { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised when a reader picked a different status beside a chapter's
    /// heading. Nothing here writes it.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<KnowledgeStatusChange> OnKnowledgeStatusChanged { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Raised with the reference a reader activated in a chapter's
    /// record.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<KnowledgeReference> OnKnowledgeNavigate { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Resolves a metadata reference to a link target.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public Func<KnowledgeReference, string?>? KnowledgeHrefFor { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Remarks against the file's blocks. Empty — the default — renders
    /// the file exactly as it did before comments existed.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public IReadOnlyList<MarkdownComment> Comments { get; set; } = [];

    /// <summary>Where the remarks are drawn when there are any. The margin is the
    /// default here because a file view is a reading surface wide enough for a
    /// column beside the prose; it falls back to inline on its own below the
    /// narrow breakpoint.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public MarkdownCommentLayout CommentLayout { get; set; } = MarkdownCommentLayout.Margin;

    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<int> OnAddComment { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<string> OnResolveComment { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<MarkdownComment> OnEditComment { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Take the full height the host gives the pane, and scroll inside
    /// it. For a file that lives in a pane or a column, where how tall it gets is
    /// the layout's business.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Fill { get; set; }

    /// <summary>How tall the region may grow before it scrolls. Any CSS length;
    /// null lets it size to its content and never scroll. Ignored when
    /// <see cref="Fill"/> is set.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? MaxHeight { get; set; } = "24rem";

    /// <summary>The scroll region's classes. The block first and always — the
    /// padding, the overflow and the scrollbars hang off it, and a modifier that
    /// replaced it would take all three with it.</summary>
    private string BodyClass =>
        ShowsEditBody ? "file-view__body file-view__body--edit" : "file-view__body";

    private string? BodyTestId => TestId is null ? null : $"{TestId}-body";

    private string? Suffix(string part) => TestId is null ? null : $"{TestId}-{part}";

    // Null omits the attribute entirely, which is the point: see the comment on
    // the region itself for why a host-supplied body gives up the tab stop. The
    // test is what is being drawn and not what was handed over, because a host
    // that supplies an editor still gets the pane's own body while the reader is
    // comparing — and that one scrolls.
    private string? BodyTabIndex => SuppliedBody is not null ? null : "0";

    /// <summary>
    /// What the scroll region is called. The file, until it is showing two
    /// versions of the file — then it is named for the relationship, which is the
    /// wording MarkdownCompareView uses and for the reason recorded there: two
    /// regions called "domain.md" in one app leave a screen-reader user no way to
    /// tell the pane from the comparison.
    /// </summary>
    private string BodyLabel =>
        ShowsComparison ? $"{Name}, {SelectedBaseline?.Label ?? "Before"} to {AfterLabel}" : Name;

    // In fill mode the flex box decides the height, so a max-height here would
    // only fight it.
    private string? BodyStyle =>
        Fill || string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(MaxHeight) ? null : $"max-height: {MaxHeight}";

    /// <summary>
    /// The layout the remarks actually get.
    /// <para>
    /// A margin is only a layout when there is something in it. A host that hands
    /// in an add handler makes every block a row whether or not anyone has said
    /// anything, and in margin mode each of those rows reserves the column — so a
    /// file nobody has remarked on would give up 16rem of its width to hold
    /// nothing, on the strength of a feature nobody has used yet. The first remark
    /// opens the column, which is also the moment it starts being worth the room.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    private MarkdownCommentLayout BodyCommentLayout =>
        Comments.Count == 0 ? MarkdownCommentLayout.Inline : CommentLayout;
}
